\; \\underbrace{(k+1)}_{\\text{sunny lines}} \\;=\\; (n-k-1)+(k+1)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, as required.\n\nAmong them the vertical lines are non‑sunny, while the \\(k+1\\) lines (3) are sunny.  \nTo obtain **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply replace the line \\(S_{k+1}\\) by any other sunny line that does **not** pass through any point of \\(T_n\\) (for instance the line \\(y = 2x + 1000\\)).  \nThis replacement does not affect the covering property, because all points of \\(T_n\\) were already covered by the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines and the vertical lines.  \nConsequently the final family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The extreme cases\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (n+1-i(i+1)),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) (which belongs to \\(T_n\\)).  \nBecause the set \\(\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) already contains one point from every “diagonal’’ of the triangle, a short verification shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \nAll slopes \\(i\\) are positive integers, hence none of the lines is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).*  \nUse the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)), or \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)), or \\(a+b=n+1\\) (hence lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \nThus the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered, and all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* The number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\) (trivial upper bound).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
