nny lines.\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nr=n-k\\qquad (r\\ge 1,\\; r\\neq 1).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(r\\ge2\\) when \\(k\\le n-2\\); the cases \\(k=0,1,n\\) are treated separately below.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  The non‑sunny backbone (\\(r\\ge2\\))\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,r-1).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le r-1\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\); hence all such points are already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  The sunny part\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = \\frac{j}{r}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{r}\\Bigr)r .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{j}{r}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n*Coverage of the remaining points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge r\\) (i.e. not covered by the vertical lines).  \nWrite \\(a=rq+s\\) with integers \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(0\\le s<r\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+k+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le r+k+1-a = (k+1)-s .\n\\]\n\nHence there exists a unique integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) such that \\(j = b+s\\).  \nA short computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_j\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{j}{r}a+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{r}\\Bigr)r\n&=\\frac{b+s}{r}(rq+s)+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{b+s}{r}\\Bigr)r  \\\\\n&=qb+bs+ \\frac{s^2}{r}+r-b-s \\\\\n&=b .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\in S_j\\). Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines (3.1) or on one of the sunny lines (3.2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Counting\n\nThe construction uses  \n\n\\[\n(r-1)\\;+\\;k \\;=\\; (n-k-1)+k \\;=\\; n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the lines \\(S_j\\)).  \nHence for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have produced a valid configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.4  Extreme values\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).  \n  Use the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  They are distinct and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).  \n  Take the non‑sunny vertical line \\(x=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)).  \n  The remaining points form the set \\(\\{(a,b):a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n  The single sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  S:\\; y = \\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\Bigr)(n-1)\n  \\]\n\n  passes through \\((n,1)\\) and, by the same calculation as in §3.2, passes through every other uncovered point.  \n  Together with the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n,\\qquad y=1\n  \\]\n\n  we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).  \n  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (n+1-i(i+1)).\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in T_n\\).  \n  Because the points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) lie on distinct “diagonals’’ of the triangle, a routine check shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \n  All slopes \\(i\\) are positive integers, so none of the lines is parallel to the forbidden directions; hence all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\n* The number of sunny lines cannot be \\(n-1\\) (Section 2).  \n* For every other integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a construction exists (Sections 3.1–3.4).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
