ct**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Definitions.**  \n   - A line is **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\)).  \n   - A line is **sunny** otherwise.  \n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   - Because we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\). Hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n   - **Step 1 (non‑sunny backbone).**  \n     Use the vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1)\n     \\]\n\n     (if \\(n-k-1\\ge 1\\)).  \n     Each \\(V_i\\) is non‑sunny and contains all points of the required set whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\).  \n   - **Step 2 (cover the remaining points).**  \n     After Step 1 the only points that may still be uncovered are those with first coordinate \\(>n-k-1\\).  \n     All such points satisfy \\(a\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently their second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le k+1\\).  \n     For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k+1\\) define the (sunny) line  \n\n     \\[\n     S_j:\\; y = \\frac{j}{\\,n-k\\,}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{\\,n-k\\,}\\Bigr)(n-k) .\n     \\]\n\n     One checks directly that \\(S_j\\) passes through the point \\((n-k,j)\\) and, because its slope \\(\\frac{j}{n-k}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), the line is sunny.  \n     Moreover, for any remaining point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n    