ontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines are non‑sunny.  Any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with\n\\(a\\le r-1\\) lies on the appropriate \\(V_i\\); the point \\((r,1)\\) lies on\n\\(H\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{r}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{r}\\Bigr)r .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(r=0\\) we replace the family \\(\\{S_j\\}\\) by any \\(k\\) distinct sunny\nlines each passing through a different point of the set\n\\(\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\); the covering argument below is\nunchanged.\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(\\frac{j}{r}>0\\) (or a very large positive slope\nwhen \\(r=0\\)), so it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor\nto \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny.\n\n#### 2.3 Covering proof  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) be a point not on any vertical line \\(V_i\\); then\n\\(a\\ge r\\).  Write \\(a=rq+s\\) with integers \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(0\\le s<r\\).\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+k+1\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le r+k+1-a=r+k+1-(rq+s)=(k+1)-s .\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(j=b+s\\).  Because \\(0\\le s<r\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), we have\n\\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(a=rq+s\\) and \\(b=j-s\\) into (3) yields\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{j}{r}a+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{r}\\Bigr)r,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_j\\).  Hence every point of\n\\(T_n\\) lies on either a vertical line \\(V_i\\) (or on \\(H\\)) or on one of\nthe sunny lines \\(S_j\\).\n\n#### 2.4 Counting  \n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(r-1)}_{\\text{vertical }V_i}\n+\\underbrace{1}_{H}\n+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }S_j}\n= n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(S_j\\)’s); the remaining \\(n-k\\) are\nnon‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Extreme cases  \n\n*All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=i\\,x+(n+1-i(i+1)),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((i,n+1-i)\\in T_n\\); a short verification shows\nthat every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of these lines.\n\n*No sunny line (\\(k=0\\)).*  \nThe construction gives the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad y=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence the point lies\non \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\) and then \\(b=1\\) (hence the point lies on \\(y=1\\)).\nThus all points are covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that\n\n* \\(k\\le n\\) (trivial upper bound);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration\n  of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and\n  containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
