\\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\qquad r:=n-k\\;( \\ge \\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rceil ).\n   \\]\n\n   We treat two cases.\n\n   *Case A: \\(r\\ge 3\\) (i.e. \\(n-k\\ge3\\)).*  \n\n   - **Non‑sunny backbone ( \\(r-1\\) lines).**  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &V_i:\\;x=i &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,r-2) &&\\text{vertical},\\\\\n     &H:\\;y=1 &&&&\\text{horizontal},\\\\\n     &D:\\;x+y=r &&&&\\text{slope }-1 .\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Sunny part ( \\(k\\) lines).** For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     S_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}\\Bigr)(r-1). \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(0<\\dfrac{j}{r-2}<1\\), the slope of \\(S_j\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   - **Covering proof.**  \n     Take any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+k+1\\)).  \n     Three mutually exclusive cases cover all possibilities.\n\n     1. **\\(a\\le r-2\\).** Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n     2. **\\(a=r-1\\) and \\(b=1\\).** This point is \\((r-1,1)\\) and lies on \\(H\\).\n\n     3. **\\(a\\ge r-1\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq(r-1,1)\\).**  \n        Write \\(a=(r-2)q+s\\) with integers \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(0\\le s\\le r-3\\).  \n        From \\(a+b\\le r+k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n        \\[\n        b\\le k+2-s .\\tag{2}\n        \\]\n\n        Set \\(j:=b+s-1\\). By (2) we have \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n        Substituting \\(a=(r-2)q+s\\) and \\(b=j+1-s\\) into (1) gives  \n\n        \\[\n        b=\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}a+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}\\Bigr)(r-1),\n        \\]\n\n        i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_j\\).  \n        Hence every required point belongs to one of the constructed lines.\n\n     The total number of lines is  \n\n     \\[\n     (r-2)+1+1+k = n ,\n     \\]\n\n     and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the lines \\