 the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, the maximal possible number of sunny lines equals \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   at least \\(n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of the lines are **non‑sunny**.  \n   Proof:  \n   – The first column \\(\\{(1,b):1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) can contain at most one point of a sunny line, because a sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) lines covering this column are non‑sunny.  \n   – The last diagonal \\(\\{(a,n+1-a):1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) has the same property, giving another lower bound \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \n   – Any line counted in both groups must be non‑sunny (it is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Adding the two bounds and subtracting the possible double count yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{non‑sunny}\\ge 2\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-n\n   =n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k=\\#\\text{sunny}\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\qquad r:=n-k\\;( \\ge \\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rceil ).\n   \\]\n\n   We treat two cases.\n\n   *Case A: \\(r\\ge 3\\) (i.e. \\(n-k\\ge3\\)).*  \n\n   - **Non‑sunny backbone ( \\(r-1\\) lines).**  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &V_i:\\;x=i &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,r-2) &&\\text{vertical},\\\\\n     &H:\\;y=1 &&&&\\text{horizontal},\\\\\n     &D:\\;x+y=r &&&&\\text{slope }-1 .\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Sunny part ( \\(k\\) lines).** For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     S_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}\\Bigr)(r-1). \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(0<\\dfrac{j}{r-2}<1\\), the slope of \\(S_j\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   - **Covering proof.**  \n     Take any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+k+1\\)).  \n     Three mutually exclusive cases cover all possibilities.\n\n     1. **\\(a\\le r-2\\).** Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n     2. **\\(a=r-1\\) and \\(b=1\\).** This point is \\((r-1,1)\\) and lies on \\(H\\).\n\n     3. **\\(a\\ge r-1\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq(r-1,1)\\).**  \n        Write \\(a=(r-2)q+s\\) with integers \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(0\\le s\\le r-3\\).  \n        From \\(a+b\\le r+k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n        \\[\n        b\\le k+2-s .\\tag{2}\n        \\]\n\n        Set \\(j:=b+s-1\\). By (2) we have \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n        Substituting \\(a=(r-2)q+s\\) and \\(b=j+1-s\\) into (1) gives  \n\n        \\[\n        b=\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}a+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{j}{\\,r-2\\,}\\Bigr)(r-1),\n        \\]\n\n        i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_j\\).  \n        Hence every required point belongs to one of the constructed lines.\n\n     The total number of lines is  \n\n     \\[\n     (r-2)+1+1+k = n ,\n     \\]\n\n     and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the lines \\(S_j\\)).  \n\n   *Case B: \\(r=2\\) (the only possibility is \\(n=3,\\;k=1\\)).*  \n   Here the general formula (1) is not applicable because \\(r-2=0\\).  \n   An explicit family of three distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) and containing exactly one sunny line is:\n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=2x-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are non‑sunny (vertical and horizontal); \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence it is sunny.  \n   The three points are covered: \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\), \\((1,2)\\) on \\(L_1\\), \\((2,1)\\) on \\(L_2\\) and also on the sunny line \\(L_3\\).  \n   Thus the case \\(r=2\\) is also realised.\n\n   Consequently for every admissible \\(k\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the universal upper bound (U); the constructions above achieve this bound for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence these are precisely the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;\nk\\text{ is admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
